{#
  Channel Ribbon — site-wide barometer strip (NOT a ticker).
  Documented in SITE_GUIDE (Channel Ribbon section) and STYLE_GUIDE §6.38.

  A slim, one-line, full-width strip directly beneath the sticky site header
  on every content page. Reads, in editorial order (position leads, price
  follows):

      ● {0.43}× trend · {near the floor} · {$64,144}

  — a pulse dot (live-gated), the live trend multiple, the canonical channel
  zone word (positionLabel), and the spot price, closing with a "see the
  dashboard →" call to action. On live-fetch fallback the dot goes static and a
  register suffix " · latest monthly data" is appended.

  TWO LINKS (dual-link refinement 2026-08-11). The data + primary CTA form one
  link to /dashboard — the destination-grade version of this read (the ribbon IS
  the rescoped site-wide entry point, so no separate chip element is built). A
  visually-secondary "the Power Law →" link follows, pointing to the foundation
  page (/the-power-law) — the dashboard is the read, the Power Law is the
  foundation that makes every page's numbers credible, so the ribbon now carries
  both, artifact-named ("see where we are" underperformed as a label — JM ruling
  2026-08-11). Because two distinct targets can't live in one <a>, the strip is a
  container with two anchors, not a single link.

  SELF-LINK SUPPRESSION. The dashboard page opts the whole ribbon out
  (channel_ribbon:false). On /the-power-law the secondary link is suppressed
  (slug guard) so no page links to itself; the dashboard link still shows.

  Layout-level like the related strip (base.njk includes it once, after the
  mobile overlay and before page content). Self-guards on a front-matter flag:
  set `channel_ribbon: false` to opt a page out (currently the homepage — its
  own bitcoin-ticker already carries this read directly under the hero — and
  /the-gallery, whose hero Power Law chart shows the same channel position).

  All values are filled at runtime by shared/channel-ribbon.js reading the same
  shared power-law-data.js helpers (plPrice / positionLabel / fetchTodayPrice)
  the trilogy pages use, so the ribbon can never disagree with a page's own
  "today" readout. Explicitly excluded by design: sticky positioning, number
  animation, 24h deltas, red/green coloring — it is a barometer, and it scrolls
  away with the page.
#}
{%- if channel_ribbon is not defined or channel_ribbon -%}
<div class="channel-ribbon" data-channel-ribbon>
  {#- Primary link: the data strip + CTA are one clickable unit → /dashboard. -#}
  <a class="cr-primary" href="/dashboard.html" aria-label="Bitcoin relative to the Power Law channel: open the Dashboard">
    <span class="cr-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    <span class="cr-metric cr-mult"><span class="cr-mult-value">&mdash;</span><span class="cr-trend-word">&nbsp;trend</span></span>
    <span class="cr-sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
    <span class="cr-metric cr-zone">&mdash;</span>
    <span class="cr-sep" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
    <span class="cr-metric cr-price">&mdash;</span>
    {#- Register suffix shown only on live-fetch fallback; JS toggles [hidden].
        Wording matches the shared todayPriceLabel/todayPriceNote helpers'
        "latest monthly data" so the ribbon can't drift from the page readouts. -#}
    <span class="cr-register" hidden>&nbsp;&middot; latest monthly data</span>
    {#- Zone divider — the quiet wall between the DATA zone and the LINK zone.
        Replaces the middot that used to sit here: a middot read as "another
        item in the list", which is exactly wrong now that what follows is a
        different kind of thing (destinations, not readings). Muted separator
        tone, never amber, so it cannot compete with the two links it fences.
        Hides with the links ≤480px (see the CSS). -#}
    <span class="cr-divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    {#- Primary CTA — the dashboard is the read. It and the divider hide
        together on very narrow viewports (the data leads there); the primary
        anchor stays clickable to /dashboard. Label mirrors the secondary
        link's artifact-named, lowercase-article form ("the Power Law →"). -#}
    <span class="cr-cta">the Dashboard&nbsp;&rarr;</span>
  </a>
  {#- Secondary link: the foundation. Suppressed on /the-power-law (no self-link)
      and hidden ≤480px (mobile keeps the dashboard link only). Visually dim / no
      orange; same separator token as the rest of the strip precedes it. -#}
  {%- if slug != 'the-power-law' -%}
  <span class="cr-sep cr-sep-secondary" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span>
  <a class="cr-secondary" href="/the-power-law.html">the Power Law&nbsp;&rarr;</a>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>
{%- endif -%}
